## 4. Module 3 Deep-Dive — Ontology (The Semantic Operating Layer)

### 4.1 Palantir Benchmark: The Ontology as the Core Innovation

This is the most critical module. The Ontology is what separates Palantir from every other data platform. It is "not a knowledge graph or data catalog" — it is an **operational layer** that maps raw data into real-world business objects (citation:15).

The Ontology uses two categories of elements (citation:15):

**Semantic Elements** — Describing the business world:
```
Object Types:  Shipment, Order, Warehouse, Customer, Carrier
Properties:    Shipment.status, Shipment.ETA, Order.priority
Links:         Shipment belongsTo Order, Order belongsTo Customer
```

**Kinetic Elements** — Changing the business world:
```
Actions:    updateShipmentETA, approveOrder, assignCarrier
Functions:  calculateDeliveryTime, predictDemand, optimizeRoute
```

Palantir's documentation advises: "Design action types around business operations, not database updates" and to "use interfaces to model shared characteristics across object types" (citation:11)(citation:12).

